    "evidence_text": "The prediction of surrounding sentences endows the model with the ability to model the sentence-level coherence.\n\nSkip-Thought  consists of an encoder and two decoders.\n\nInferSent  instead pre-trains the model in a manner of supervised learning.\n\nIt uses a large-scale NLI dataset as the\n\npre-training task to learn the sentence representation.\n\nMethod\tOur method follows the same input format and the model architecture with original BERT.\n\nThe proposed method solely concerns the NSP task.\n\nAs reported , the final pre-trained model is able to achieve 97%-98% accuracy on the NSP task.\n\nsuggest the order discrimination is essential for BERT pre-training.\n\nAnother advantage of PSP is to enhance document-level supervision.\n\nNote that these in-adjacent sentences will introduce much more training noise to the model.\n\nTherefore, the label smoothing technique is adopted to reduce the noise of these additional samples.\n\nIt achieves this by relaxing our confidence on the labels, e.g., transforming the target probability from (1.0,\n\n0.0) to (0.8, 0.2) in a binary classification problem.\n\nExperiment Settings\tThis section gives detailed experiment settings.\n\nTo accelerate the training speed, two-phase training  is adopted.\n\nThe numbers of training steps of two phases are 50K and 40K for the BERTBase model.\n\nWe used AdamW  optimizer with a learning rate of 1e-4, a $\\beta _1$ of\n\nThe first 10% of the total steps are used for learning rate warming up, followed by the linear decay schema.\n\nWe used a dropout probability of 0.1 on all layers.\n\nWe explore three method settings for comparison.\n\nThe label distribution is 1:1:1.\n\n(IsNextInadj) label is mapped to IsPrev (IsNext) with a label smoothing factor of 0.8.\n\nBERT-PN is used to verify the feasibility of PSP.\n\nThe comparison with BERT-PN5cls illustrates whether more document-level information helps.\n\nThen we provide experimental results on the NLI and MRC tasks to verify the effectiveness of the proposed method.\n\nAt last, the proposed method is evaluated on several Chinese datasets.\n\nOrder-invariant with PSP\tNSP in the pre-training is useful for NLI and MRC task .\n\nTo verify our assumption, a primary experiment was conducted.\n\nwas conducted for 3 epochs for all the tasks, with a learning rate of 2e-5.\n\nThe predictions were obtained by evaluating the training checkpoint with the best validation performance.\n\nBesides NLI, our model also performs better than BERTBase in the STS task.\n\nActually, the proposed method has a positive effect whenever the input is a sentence pair.\n\nThe improvement should be attributed to better semantic representation.\n\nThis indicates that including a broader range of the context is effective for improving inference ability.\n\nConsidering that the representation of IsNext and IsNextInadj should be coherent, we propose\n\n0.3% increase in EM can be obtained.\n\nTABREF26.\n\nThis is one of the most challenging MRC datasets that require sophisticated reasoning.\n\nThe model computes for a concatenation a scalar as the score, which is then used in a softmax layer for the final prediction.\n\nis to identify whether the sentence contains the answer to the question.\n\nCMRC-2018 is the Chinese Machine Reading Comprehension dataset.\n\nDRCD  is also a Chinese MRC data set.\n\nThe data follows the format of SQuAD.\n\nFor Chinese NLP tasks, we pre-train the model using Chinese corpus.\n\nFor the first 3 Chinese tasks, we follow the settings as in ERNIE .\n\nOur method achieves comparable or even better results against ERNIE 2.0 .\n\nNote that the Chinese ERNIE 2.0 is equipped with 5 different\n\nNSP task to provide more document-level information in the pre-training.\n\nDespite the simplicity, extensive experiments demonstrate that the model obtains a better ability in pair-wise semantic reasoning.\n\nIn the future, we will investigate the way to take advantages of both large-scale training and our method.",
